<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cartography">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cartography</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cartography is the science and art of making maps. Portuguese navigators of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries greatly expanded European geographical knowledge and charts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> cartography, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, advanced substantially through the demands of managing the global trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cartography.jpg" alt="Cartography" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Kepler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kepler-world.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>What counts as the earliest known map is debated, partly because the term map is not well defined and some artifacts that might be maps may be something else. A wall painting that might depict the ancient Anatolian city of Çatalhöyük has been dated to the late 7th millennium BCE, and prehistoric alpine rock carvings at Mount Bego in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Valcamonica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, dated to the 4th millennium BCE, are widely interpreted as showing cultivated plots.</p><p>Other maps of the ancient world include the Minoan House of the Admiral wall painting of about 1600 BCE, showing a seaside community in oblique perspective, and an engraved map of the Babylonian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> from the Kassite period. The oldest surviving world maps are from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> of the 9th century BCE; one shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, Urartu and several cities, all encircled by a bitter river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Cartography is the study and practice of making and using maps, combining science, aesthetics and technique. Its traditional objectives are to select the traits to be mapped, to represent terrain on flat media through map projections, to remove irrelevant characteristics through generalisation, and to arrange the elements of the map to convey its message.</p><p>Maps are divided into general maps, made for a general audience, and thematic maps, which concern specific geographic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>, such as a map of corn production. A topographic map describes a place, with contour lines showing elevation, while a topological map disregards scale and detail to communicate route information; Beck's London Underground map is the usual example.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>About 1,100 medieval European mappae mundi survive, of which some 900 illustrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. In 1154 the Arab geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">Muhammad al-Idrisi</a> produced his atlas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-rogeriana">Tabula Rogeriana</a>, and his world map, mostly Ptolemaic in conception, remained the most accurate for the next three centuries. In 1492 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-behaim">Martin Behaim</a>, advisor to King John II of Portugal, made the oldest extant globe, and Hartmann Schedel was among the first to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press for maps.</p><p>The Selden map, made by a Chinese cartographer around 1620, though historians debate the date, shows trading routes, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> rose and a scale bar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> maps used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate intaglio rather than woodcut, and mapmakers often plagiarised: Herman Moll's Beaver Map of 1715 closely reproduces Nicolas de Fer's work of 1698. Some scholars argue that maps are inherently biased, and that cartography served imperial power in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cartography” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch <b>cartography</b>, navigation and commercial law advanced substantially through the demands of managing a global network, and Hugo Grotius's Mare Liberum (1609), written in defence of the VOC, became a founding text of international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese culture produced the Manueline style of the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém, the epic Os Lusíadas of Luís de Camões, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and <b>cartography</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…securing the overland routes under the Pax Mongolica, it allowed goods, ideas and people to travel between China, the Middle East and Europe, from merchants such as Marco Polo to envoys such as the Franciscan William of Rubruck, and encouraged exchanges in astronomy, medicine, <b>cartography</b> and gunpowder technology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese <b>cartography</b>, navigational science and maritime technology spread across Europe and gave later Spanish, Dutch, English and French ventures a technical foundation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Several hundred Catholic missionaries reached China before 1724, when the Yongzheng Emperor proscribed Christianity as a “heterodox teaching”; the Jesuits among them served the court in gunnery, <b>cartography</b> and astronomy.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>Gotha was a centre of <b>cartography</b> with the Justus Perthes firm and of insurance, while Coburg was a court town.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a></li></ul></section>
